>> title: Red Bean Paste
>> category: Custards & Puddings
>> tags: adzuki, sweet, filling, stovetop, make-ahead, vegan
>> counters: Bakery, Dim Sum Counter
>> aka: anko, tsubuan, koshian, tsubu-an, koshi-an, azuki paste, adzuki bean paste, hong dou sha, dou sha, sweet red bean paste
>> pairs-with: japanese-milk-bread, lotus-seed-paste
>> servings: 16
>> time: 3 hr 15 min
>> slack: narrow — by the last twenty-five minutes the pan is mostly sugar and it scorches without warning, and a scorched batch is all of it
>> step.1: boil 5 min, drain the first water
>> step.2: simmer 90 min
>> step.3: mash chunky, or sieve smooth
>> step.4: cook down with the sugar 25 min
>> step.5: beat in the oil, cool 1 hr

Cover @dried adzuki beans{2%cup}(400 g) with @water for the first boil{6%cup}(1.4 L) in a #saucepan{}, bring to a ~boil{5%min}, then drain.

Return @&(~1)beans{} to the pan with @water for the simmer{8%cup}(1.9 L) and ~simmer{90%min}, topping up so they stay just covered, until a bean crushes to nothing between two fingers.

Mash @&(~1)tender beans{} in what liquid is left: roughly with a masher for chunky tsubu-an, or through a sieve with the skins thrown out for smooth koshi-an.

Cook @&(~1)mashed beans{} down with @granulated sugar{1 1/2%cup}(300 g) and @fine sea salt{1/4%tsp}, stirring so it does not catch, ~simmer{25%min} until a spoon drawn across the pan leaves a furrow that holds.

Beat @neutral oil{2%Tbs}(30 mL) into @&(~1)paste{} for gloss, then ~cool{1%hr}.
